# Independent chapter 01 browser and saved-snapshot verification

Fixed production checkpoint: 9c5883b555ebe5e09380ea4ab20ce275d0687259. The reviewer executed independently authored browser probes, not a copy of root's results. No source, alias, renderer, registry, production or original artifacts were edited. This is a bounded display/navigation and restoration review; the reviewer authored the original chapter 01 manuscript, so this is not an independent full semantic review of that manuscript.

## Output scope correction

Direct inspection of fixed tools/src/doc/canonical.rs::html confirms that aliases are not passed to HTML generation. GitHub Markdown preserves eight legacy fragments plus seven semantic n-Section anchors. HTML exposes the seven semantic anchors only. The previous collision record's suggestion that both outputs require existing legacy aliases was based on an incorrect premise, now explicitly corrected in ../review-canonical01-collision/record.md. That manifest is 5881399c6337fb7506b9e7512c502c16d760df9d14b5dbad49acde15a2253307. The original failed DOM snapshot remains a failure. No HTML legacy-alias acceptance is inferred here.

## Executed checks

- GitHub fixed blob URL: https://github.com/neknaj/NEPL3/blob/9c5883b555ebe5e09380ea4ab20ce275d0687259/doc/spec/01-architecture.md . Actual returned application rawLines reconstruct exact Git Markdown bytes, including LF. The independently fixed original eight legacy fragments and all seven Section IDs are tested separately from the current alias list. All 15 targets were unique, navigated from a remote scroll position, and stabilized visibly over five repeated samples. The former bootstrap collision is absent after the clarified heading. Actual DOM and full screenshot are saved.
- HTML bundles first and second were copied into reviewer-owned /independent/deep/first and /independent/deep/second paths. Both six-file bundles are byte-identical. Each manifest file digest is checked, and all four manifest source digests match their actual source Git blobs at the fixed commit. This ties the copied artifacts to the fixed sources; the reviewer did not rebuild the Rust generator.
- Chromium 151.0.7922.34, Firefox 153.0, WebKit 26.5 on Windows 11; Playwright 1.62.0 / Python 3.13.12. At 375 and 1280 pixels, both bundle copies passed with document JavaScript disabled: 12 cases, 84 actual semantic fragment navigations, and 12 clicked links reaching the real chapter 22 document. All target IDs are unique, legacy Markdown aliases absent, language ja, eight headings including the clarified bootstrap title, CSS active, Ruby present, no page-level horizontal overflow, and both code blocks exactly equal the old Markdown code bytes including final LF. Requests stayed local with no failures. evaluate calls are test instrumentation, not document script execution.
- The displayed chapter 01 contains its original meaningful paragraphs, code blocks and annotations. WebKit narrow/wide screenshots and GitHub screenshot were visually inspected. Text and readings remain visible; the long dependency Anno makes a tall line box and narrower wrapping, as allowed by the current box-based backend. No overlapping or clipped ordinary text was observed. Code blocks retain their full DOM bytes and their overflow containers; this does not claim a human accessibility or all-user readability audit.
- The README restoration Python fence was extracted byte-for-byte and executed without editing its paths/code in a separate reviewer workspace. Exactly six selected root-html-first files were restored; all equal the original generated bundle bytes and listed hashes. File-URL viewing then passed Chromium/Firefox/WebKit with document JavaScript disabled and HTTP(S) requests actively blocked. CSS applied via computed style and the real chapter 22 relative link worked offline in all three engines. No Rust/Node/compiler/network operation is in the restoration snippet.

## Probe and environment limitations retained

Connected browser runtime setup returned No browser is available and an empty browser list; the existing project cross-engine Playwright environment was used for these independent tests. Environment records hash each launcher path returned by Playwright; Chromium's recorded chrome.exe hash is not claimed to identify the separate headless child executable. Browser versions are actual launched versions.

The first offline probe tried to read file-origin stylesheet cssRules and stopped with Chromium SecurityError, before navigation success. The verbatim restoration itself had already succeeded. That probe/script/log and empty result are preserved as bootstrap-before-*; this is a test-harness origin restriction, not a stylesheet failure. The corrected probe uses actual computed style, creates a fresh workspace, executes the same unmodified README snippet, and completes all engines. No browser-security flag was weakened.

The initial online run's PowerShell console log has non-ASCII display transcoding in progress labels; fixed source/JSON/HTML and result fragment strings are UTF-8 bytes and are the identity evidence. That raw log is retained, not silently normalized. Scratch working copies for serving and restoration are excluded from the review payload manifest; their file paths, sizes and hashes are in input-hashes.json and bootstrap-results.json. The actual original bundle and archive payloads are maintained by root. Root generation/CI evidence is not represented as reviewer-executed runtime tests.

All requested browser and restoration checks completed without a new blocking finding. This does not establish Pages deployment, screen-reader human operation, arbitrary Markdown engines, arbitrary datasets, all-platform reproducibility, the entire T21 migration, or repository separation.
